Label the radius of the circle with center $C$ as $a$. Draw a diagram and represent the radius of the smaller circle by a dashed line; we must ignore it. Let $O(0,0)$, $D(3+1,0)$, and $A$ be points on the diagram, as labeled. Then $\triangle{\big(D, A, O\big)} \sim \triangle{\big(D, A, C\big)}$, and by SAS, $\triangle{\big(D, A, O\big)} \sim \triangle{\big(D, B, C\big)}$. Therefore, the ratio of the corresponding sides is the same, which gives us $C(6-?=2a)$. Solving the equation, we find that $a=1$ or $a=\dfrac{3}{2}$. The sum is $\boxed{\dfrac{5}{2}}$. <answer>.

A circle with center $C$ is tangent to the positive $x$ and $y$-axes and externally tangent to the circle centered at $(3,0)$ with radius $1$. What is the sum of all possible radii of the circle with center $C$?
The following is a bit simpler and shorter proof.
[asy]
size(250);
draw((0,0)--(0,10)--(10,10)--(10,0)--cycle);
draw(circle(O,1));
draw((0,0)--(-3,13));
pair[] X={(-2,4)};
X[0]=intersectionpoint(cilarity of the two circles with the tangent);
label("$x$",(10,0),S);
label("$y$",(0,10),W);
for(int i=1; i < size(X); ++i){
label("$(X_{i},Y_{i})$",X[i],NE);
}
[/asy]
Now, let $R$ be the radius of circle with center the origin (so our answer is $R$), as drawn in the diagram. Note that $A$ and $B$ lie on that circle, and so their $y$ coordinates are $\pm R$. Furthermore, $A$ lies on $Cx$ since $\angle ACX=90^{\circ}$ due to the right triangle in the diagram.
Therefore, our desired radius is equal to $1+13-5\sqrt{5}=5(\sqrt{5}-1).$
Use the reflection to obtain $\overline{AC}=13$ and $\overline{AO}=\overline{AB}=5.$ Now, apply the Pythagorean theorem for right triangle $\triangle{ADB}:$
\begin{align*}
5^{2}+5^{2}&=13^{2}\rightarrow
25+25&=169\rightarrow
50&=1(c+a)^{2}\rightarrow
a^{2}+10a+25&=169.
\end{align*}
The solutions to the last equation are $a=5$ or $a=-15$. But since $r$ must be positive, we have $r=5.$

